CONCLUSION :

The entire blame to be placed on the algorithm is
completely wrong when they should be placed the least
amount of blame out of all factors.

The dominant faulty parties are:
1. 0/1 encoding of symptoms
2. Inevitable overlapping of symptoms
3. Non inclusive of distinguishing clinical factors like: severity, duration and context

The input is solely based on the presence or absence
of symptoms, completely ignoring the very clinical factors that
are used by physicians to clarify ambiguousness.

The input doesn't care about how long the symptoms have persisted,
if the disease had happened in the past and how severe the symptoms are.
It only says, "You have Symptom X so you have Disease X."

In real medicine, symptoms WILL be common among multiple diseases
so this ambiguousness is inevitable.

With this many flaws in the input, the algorithms
are destined to give wrong predictions. The poor algorithms
will obviously give out from what they're fed on.

So, the diagnosing of disease while giving in the input
on the basis of presence/absence of symptoms, no distinguishing
clinical factors and overlapping symptoms, will make even the most
"perfect" and "sophisticated" algorithm give out
incorrect predictions.




